// EXPECT-DIAG: E048
// EXPECT-DIAG: 'MathUtil' is a struct type, not an instance
// Regression test: calling a struct method via the bare struct TYPE NAME
// (no instance) must be a clean compile error, not a crash.
//
// Before this fix, Codegen's getExprType() had no case at all for a bare
// struct type name used as a static-call qualifier (Var->IsStructName,
// set by the Resolver) -- none of its variable/symbol lookups could ever
// find it, so it fell through to `return nullptr`, which propagated to a
// `report_fatal_error("could not determine type of method call base...
// this is a compiler bug")` in MethodCallExprAST::codegen(), crashing the
// WHOLE COMPILER PROCESS. No test in the suite exercised this at all.
//
// The crash is now fixed (getExprType() correctly resolves the struct
// type via GlobalSymbolTable). This test now specifically pins down a
// DIFFERENT, still-true fact: a `fn StructName.method(...)` declaration
// (dot form) is always an INSTANCE method -- ParseFuncDef() unconditionally
// injects a `self` parameter for it, with no opt-out -- so calling it via
// the bare struct TYPE NAME with '.' syntax (no instance) can never satisfy
// that required parameter and always fails with a wrong-arity error,
// regardless of what arguments are given. Calling the SAME method through a
// real instance (`instance.method(...)`) works fine (see the companion
// non-fail test using an instance instead).
//
// True static methods DO now exist as a separate, deliberate declaration
// form: `fn StructName::method(...)` (the '::' form -- no `self` injected
// at all), callable only as `StructName::method(args)`. See
// tests/07_functions_modules/scope_static_method*.tr for that feature's own
// regression tests. This file is unaffected by that feature's existence: it
// specifically exercises the OLD dot-declared instance method called the
// wrong way, not the new syntax.

struct MathUtil {}

fn MathUtil.square(x: int): int {
    return x * x;
}

fn main(): int {
    mut y: int = MathUtil.square(5);
    return 0;
}
